course: dictionary
lesson: 1234
title: Bullying: hurting on purpose
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Bullying is when someone hurts, frightens or leaves out another person on purpose, again and again. The bully often thinks they are stronger. The clues are in the context: «Bullying usually goes on until someone tells an adult who can stop it.» It keeps going, so it needs a grown-up to stop it.
explain: Look for the clues on purpose and again and again. «A single argument between friends is not the same as bullying.» One argument between equals is a disagreement, not bullying. Ask yourself: is it deliberate, and does it keep happening?
explain: Bullying can take different forms. It can be physical, like hitting or pushing, or verbal, like name-calling and teasing. It can be social, like leaving someone out or spreading rumours, or online, which is called cyberbullying.
explain: Bullying is never the victim's fault. «Hitting back can get you hurt and can make the bullying worse.» The safer choice is to tell a trusted adult and to stay with friends.
fact: Bullying means hurting, frightening or leaving out someone on purpose, again and again.
fact: «Bullying is hurting someone again and again who finds it hard to defend themselves; it is always wrong.»
fact: Bullying can be physical, verbal, social or online.
fact: If bullying happens to you, tell a trusted adult and keep evidence if it is online.
fact: Bystanders can help by not joining in and by reporting what they see.
exercise: «Bullying usually goes on until someone tells an adult who can stop it.» What does this tell us? | It only happens online | It is a kind of game | It stops by itself after one day | *It keeps going unless an adult steps in
exercise: «A single argument between friends is not the same as bullying.» Why is one argument not bullying? | It is online | It always happens on purpose again and again | *It is a one-off between equals | It is about being stronger
exercise: Ella says bullying is sometimes the victim's fault. What is the right answer? | Yes, if the victim is different | *No, bullying is never the victim's fault | Yes, if the victim hits back | Yes, if the victim is quiet
test: What does the word bullying mean? | Arguing once about a toy | *Hurting, frightening or leaving out someone on purpose, again and again | Playing a fair game with friends | Helping someone who is sad
test: What is the best thing to do if you are being bullied? | *Tell a trusted adult and stay with friends | Stay silent and hope it stops | Hit back as hard as you can | Post a mean message about the bully
exam: Which statement about bullying is true? | Bullying is sometimes fine if the other person is weak | *Bullying is always wrong, and it is never the victim's fault | Bullying only happens in the playground | Bullying is a one-off argument between equals
